---
title: Real Youth Employment
order: 2
icon: briefcase
---

Nairobi's youth unemployment rate stands at 39 percent — the highest in the country. Every morning, 600,000 young people with diplomas, degrees, and certificates hit the streets searching for work that does not exist. The *jua kali* sector absorbs many, but offers no security, no benefits, and no path to a dignified life. The county government has treated youth employment as an afterthought — sporadic internships and empty promises while the crisis deepens.

We will build a comprehensive youth employment strategy rooted in partnership with TVET institutions. The Nairobi Youth Skills Fund will co-finance industry-aligned training in construction, digital technology, renewable energy, hospitality, and the creative economy. Every training program will end in a certified apprenticeship of no less than six months with a registered employer — the county will cover 50 percent of the apprentice's stipend.

For young entrepreneurs, we will open a one-stop business registration centre that issues permits within 48 hours. We will launch a county startup grant programme targeting green economy and technology businesses, and establish affordable workspaces in every sub-county. The Nairobi Enterprise Board will provide mentorship, market linkages, and low-interest credit to youth-led enterprises. The *kazi kwa kazi* casual labour model — exploitation dressed as opportunity — will be phased out in favour of formal, benefits-protected employment.

Every shilling spent on youth programmes will be tracked and published on a Youth Employment Dashboard — real-time data on training placements, apprenticeship uptake, start-up funding disbursed, and jobs created. The young people of Nairobi do not want handouts. They want a government that takes their future as seriously as they do. We will deliver it.
